WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.470
Last term our science club trained a small

2
00:00:03.470 --> 00:00:05.950
model to sort mangoes from limes.

3
00:00:06.580 --> 00:00:09.580
It worked, until one group typed in

4
00:00:09.580 --> 00:00:11.980
twenty wrong labels, just as a joke.

5
00:00:12.570 --> 00:00:14.890
We trained it again, and it started

6
00:00:14.890 --> 00:00:17.170
calling big mangoes limes.

7
00:00:18.430 --> 00:00:22.270
Today: poisoned data, how to find it, and

8
00:00:22.270 --> 00:00:24.450
how to keep your training data clean.

9
00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:28.160
A model learns only from its examples.

10
00:00:28.950 --> 00:00:32.010
So if someone slips wrong or planted

11
00:00:32.010 --> 00:00:34.390
examples into the training data, it

12
00:00:34.390 --> 00:00:35.470
learns the wrong thing.

13
00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:37.160
That's data poisoning.

14
00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:40.180
The attacker never touches the model:

15
00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:42.140
spoiling its data is enough.

16
00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:44.920
Where can it happen at school?

17
00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:47.460
Data scraped from the internet,

18
00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:49.220
which anyone can edit.

19
00:00:49.870 --> 00:00:51.410
Labels added by strangers.

20
00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:55.140
And data collected by pupils, where one

21
00:00:55.140 --> 00:00:58.160
careless or mischievous entry slips in.

22
00:00:59.320 --> 00:01:01.540
A sneakier kind is a backdoor.

23
00:01:02.220 --> 00:01:04.720
The model behaves normally, until it

24
00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:07.380
sees a hidden trigger, like a special

25
00:01:07.380 --> 00:01:08.900
word or a small sticker.

26
00:01:09.550 --> 00:01:11.030
Then it gives the answer

27
00:01:11.030 --> 00:01:12.030
the attacker chose.

28
00:01:13.350 --> 00:01:15.670
So how do you find poison?

29
00:01:16.430 --> 00:01:18.790
Look for examples that sit far

30
00:01:18.790 --> 00:01:21.270
from their own kind: a mango that

31
00:01:21.270 --> 00:01:23.430
weighs forty grams is odd.

32
00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:26.580
Compare each one with the middle value

33
00:01:26.580 --> 00:01:29.520
of its kind, the median, because one

34
00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:31.360
bad value barely moves it.

35
00:01:32.530 --> 00:01:33.570
Here it is in Python.

36
00:01:34.410 --> 00:01:36.890
Sort the weights and take the middle one.

37
00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:39.780
Then flag any weight further

38
00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:41.400
than half the median away.

39
00:01:42.140 --> 00:01:44.920
The forty-gram mango shows up at once.

40
00:01:46.280 --> 00:01:46.760
Quick check!

41
00:01:47.370 --> 00:01:48.810
The odd-one-out check

42
00:01:48.810 --> 00:01:50.690
flags twelve examples.

43
00:01:51.580 --> 00:01:53.580
Should we just delete all twelve?

44
00:01:54.840 --> 00:01:55.380
Look first.

45
00:01:56.150 --> 00:01:57.970
The check also flags honest

46
00:01:57.970 --> 00:02:00.610
examples, like a really small mango.

47
00:02:01.340 --> 00:02:04.020
Remove what's truly wrong, and write

48
00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:05.660
down what you removed and why.

49
00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:08.160
Cleaning data always costs

50
00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:09.180
a few good examples.

51
00:02:10.580 --> 00:02:12.980
The best defences come before training.

52
00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:15.480
Know where every example came

53
00:02:15.480 --> 00:02:17.940
from, and keep a clean test set

54
00:02:17.940 --> 00:02:19.260
that nobody can change.

55
00:02:19.890 --> 00:02:22.590
Compare the score on it before and after

56
00:02:22.590 --> 00:02:25.470
new data: a sudden drop is a warning.

57
00:02:26.750 --> 00:02:28.930
In the app, flip some labels and

58
00:02:28.930 --> 00:02:31.390
train: watch the accuracy fall.

59
00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:34.860
Then press “Find the odd ones out”,

60
00:02:34.900 --> 00:02:36.460
drop them and train again.

61
00:02:37.070 --> 00:02:38.350
Most of the damage goes,

62
00:02:38.850 --> 00:02:40.250
but not all of it.

63
00:02:41.440 --> 00:02:42.200
Your turn.

64
00:02:42.820 --> 00:02:45.240
Pupils add a hundred new photos to

65
00:02:45.240 --> 00:02:47.320
our fruit data, and the score on our

66
00:02:47.320 --> 00:02:49.080
clean test set drops from

67
00:02:49.080 --> 00:02:50.940
ninety-seven to eighty percent.

68
00:02:51.640 --> 00:02:52.160
What now?

69
00:02:53.410 --> 00:02:56.070
Stop and check the new photos: a

70
00:02:56.070 --> 00:02:57.650
sudden drop is a warning.

71
00:02:58.310 --> 00:02:59.890
Remember: wrong examples

72
00:02:59.890 --> 00:03:02.350
in, wrong model out.

73
00:03:03.020 --> 00:03:05.220
Find what sits far from its kind,

74
00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:08.200
look before you delete, and guard

75
00:03:08.200 --> 00:03:09.780
your data before you train.

76
00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:13.300
Try it in class tomorrow: pupils

77
00:03:13.300 --> 00:03:15.880
measure fruit, leaves or stones,

78
00:03:16.460 --> 00:03:17.560
and label each one.

79
00:03:18.130 --> 00:03:20.770
In secret, you swap three labels.

80
00:03:21.550 --> 00:03:23.490
Groups find the median of each

81
00:03:23.490 --> 00:03:25.570
kind and hunt for the poison.
